Return to our four servers. If their rack PDU or upstream circuit is the constraint, spare building capacity cannot bypass it. Installing a larger PDU alone also fails if the wiring feeding it remains undersized. The entire required path must support the load.

Why density changes the hardware

Putting more compute in one rack concentrates electrical demand. Voltage describes an electrical potential difference; current is the flow of charge. For a given delivered power, raising voltage allows less current, holding other relevant factors constant.

That matters because current heats conductors. Lower current can reduce electrical losses and the conductor material required for a given design. This helps explain why dense AI systems require deliberate choices about voltage, connectors, cables and distribution—not simply more outlets.

There is another physical bill to pay. Almost all electricity used by IT equipment ultimately becomes heat. A rack consuming around 50 kW therefore requires heat removal of roughly that scale. Electrical capacity and cooling capacity must be available at the same location.

Keeping the rack alive when something fails

The equipment also has to work when conditions are imperfect. NVIDIA’s DGX B200 uses six power supplies in a 5+1 arrangement: one can fail without interrupting normal operation. Additional losses can reduce performance or cause shutdown, depending on how many supplies remain powered.

Redundancy means providing an alternative component or path to survive a specified failure. It is capacity with a job already assigned to it.

Consider a separate illustrative rack with two independent 40 kW feeds. If either feed must carry the entire rack after the other fails, is this an 80 kW rack?

Answer: It can support at most 40 kW under that requirement, before other limits and design margins. Loading it to 60 kW across both feeds may work normally, but one surviving feed cannot support that load. Adding the ratings has counted backup capacity as expansion capacity.

The failure requirement determines the usable capacity. The next lesson will examine whether those supposedly independent paths really are independent.

Can software buy us room?

Power capping limits how much a system may draw. It can help fit equipment within an electrical budget, but may reduce performance. The useful question is how completed work changes relative to power consumption.

If power falls substantially while output falls only slightly, a cap may improve work per unit of electricity. If the workload slows sharply, it may simply turn an electrical constraint into a longer, more expensive job. Measure an equivalent workload; a lower power number alone proves little.

This connects back to Lesson 048. A synchronized training job can wait for its slowest required workers. Restricting one server’s power can therefore affect useful output beyond that server.

What would actually solve the deployment?

Suppose we upgrade the electrical path, but the rack’s cooling remains insufficient. Have we unlocked the four servers?

Answer: Not at their intended sustained load. We have moved the bottleneck. Heat removal must improve, equipment must be spread across suitable racks, or operation must fit the remaining limits.

The supply chain must deliver a working combination: power supplies, distribution units, conductors, connectors, monitoring and cooling. A supplier’s product matters when it removes the binding constraint reliably; rising rack power alone does not tell us who captures the profit.

From hardware to useful compute → Enough power at every stage, enough capacity after the specified failure, and enough cooling at the rack.

Before accepting a capacity claim, ask where power is available, how much survives a failure, and whether cooling supports the same load.

